---
import Layout from '../layouts/PortfolioLayout.astro';
import { profile, skills } from '../data/portfolio';
const deskSketch =
  '   ___________________\n  |  >_               |\n  |  build.           |\n  |  understand.      |\n  |  try again.       |\n  |___________________|\n          |___|\n       ___|___|___';
---

<Layout
  title="About — Riccardo Salzmann"
  description="Software engineer, University of Michigan graduate, Linux tinkerer, and former JV soccer coach. Get to know Riccardo Salzmann."
>
  <div class="content-shell">
    <header class="content-header about-intro">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">THE PERSON BEHIND THE DESK</p>
        <h1>
          I’m Riccardo.
          <br />
          <span>I like figuring things out.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="lede">
          I’m a software engineer at SportsMEDIA Technology in Durham, North
          Carolina, and a freelance engineer for Quantum America. My work moves
          between real-time graphics, interactive 3D applications, and systems
          connected to the physical world.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="about-photo">
        <div class="photo-label">FIELD NOTES / BEHIND THE BROADCAST</div>
        <img
          src="/media/smt/riccardo-broadcast.webp"
          srcset="/media/smt/riccardo-broadcast-small.webp 640w, /media/smt/riccardo-broadcast.webp 1200w"
          sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 520px"
          width="1200"
          height="900"
          fetchpriority="high"
          alt="Riccardo Salzmann seated at a broadcast production console, surrounded by race feeds and monitors."
        />
        <figcaption>
          <strong>From my desk to the broadcast.</strong>
          <span>Graphics, data, and the people bringing them to air.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </header>
    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="about-copy">
        <section>
          <h2>A few different places to build.</h2>
          <p>
            At SMT, I work on the systems behind live sports broadcasts:
            rendering geometry, synchronizing data and video, and making useful
            tools for operators. At Quantum America, I built an interactive 3D
            web application with TypeScript, React, and Three.js, along with
            tools for visual content and connected displays.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before that, I worked across EDAG internships and co-op terms in
            Michigan and Germany, building automotive interfaces, working with
            CAN systems, and configuring embedded Linux applications. I
            graduated from the University of Michigan with a B.S. in Computer
            Science in December 2024.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">HOW I WORK</p>
          <h2>From the first question to the person using it.</h2>
          <p>
            My NASCAR telemetry project began with interviews with project and
            operations leads. I carried it through data analysis, 2D and 3D
            views, report exports, and a standalone installer. At SMT, I also
            work with operators on calibration and provide on-call support when
            a live broadcast needs a fix.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/work/smt/#nascar-telemetry">
            Follow the telemetry project →
          </a>
          <p>
            I build tools that other engineers can reuse: a shared
            line-rendering library at SMT, firmware update tooling at EDAG, and
            Blender automation with Python and Node.js for Quantum. I also work
            on rendering performance and automated tests, so the software stays
            responsive and consistent as it grows.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/work/quantum/#performance-quality">
            Explore the freelance work →
          </a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">WORKING WITH PEOPLE</p>
          <h2>Research, explain, and share what works.</h2>
          <p>
            At EDAG, I interviewed electrical and electronics teams to identify
            possible generative AI applications. I led the prompt-engineering
            and market-analysis work, helped develop educational material, and
            discussed technical options with software teams and department
            leaders. At SMT, I introduced the team to Claude Code with CLI
            demonstrations and use cases.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/work/edag/#generative-ai-research">
            Read about the EDAG initiative →
          </a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <p class="kicker">AWAY FROM THE KEYBOARD</p>
          <h2>A different kind of team.</h2>
          <p>
            From August through October 2025, I was head coach of the junior
            varsity soccer team at East Chapel Hill High School. I led a
            23-player team through a 24-game season and planned and coached
            training sessions and games.
          </p>
          <p>
            I speak English natively and German fluently. At my own workbench, I
            enjoy tinkering with computers, building custom tools, and
            customizing Linux systems.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
      <aside class="personal-card">
        <p class="kicker">A FEW CONSTANTS</p>
        <pre aria-hidden="true">{deskSketch}</pre>
        <h2>
          Graphics. Systems.
          <br />
          Things you can explore.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Computer science at Michigan.
          <br />
          Internships in the US and Germany.
          <br />
          Live broadcasts at SMT.
          <br />
          Interactive places at Quantum.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/work/">
          Explore the work →
        </a>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <section>
      <p class="kicker">THE TOOLBOX</p>
      {skills.map((group) => (
        <div class="skill-group">
          <h3>{group.name}</h3>
          <ul class="skill-tags">
            {group.items.map((item) => (
              <li>{item}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </section>
    <section class="contact-block" id="contact">
      <p class="kicker">LET’S CONNECT</p>
      <h2>Have something interesting in mind?</h2>
      <p>
        For engineering opportunities, collaboration, or a conversation about
        the work:
      </p>
      <a class="contact-email" href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>
        {profile.email}
      </a>
      <div class="action-row">
        <a
          class="site-button"
          href={profile.linkedin}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          LinkedIn ↗
        </a>
        <a
          class="site-button secondary"
          href={profile.github}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          GitHub ↗
        </a>
        <a class="site-button secondary" href="/resume/">
          Résumé →
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-intro {
    max-width: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 44px;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 58px;
  }
  .about-intro h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 3.4vw, 46px);
  }
  .about-intro .lede {
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .about-photo {
    margin: 0;
    border: 1px solid #3e4651;
    background: #0c1016;
  }
  .photo-label {
    padding: 15px 18px;
    color: #a6c4d1;
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .about-photo img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .about-photo figcaption {
    padding: 19px 20px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 9px;
  }
  .about-photo strong {
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: #d3dfe6;
  }
  .about-photo span {
    font-size: 11px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #97a8b5;
  }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .about-intro {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 22px;
      align-items: start;
    }
    .about-intro h1 {
      font-size: clamp(30px, 6vw, 46px);
    }
  }
</style>
